Subject: [PATCH] feat(steering): causal activation steering in the gemma4 forward

Adds --enable-steering. The server loads a bank of steering directions, a request names one of its features, and the model adds alpha * v to the residual stream leaving --steering-layer.

    h -> h + alpha * v

layers/steering.py holds the arithmetic: static_steer for the add, conditional_steer for the add under a probe and a threshold, and apply_steering as the one call a model makes. Static steering is conditional steering at a threshold of -inf, so both ride one code path and one program.

The hook site is the only thing that compiles in. It's a Python int on the inner model, set before nnx.split() bakes it into model_def, the same way layers_to_capture works. Everything a request chooses arrives as a device array on ForwardBatch: the bank [S, D], a bank row per token, alpha per token and a threshold per token. So one executable covers every feature, every alpha, every mix of static and conditional, and every choice of steered tokens, and two requests in one batch can steer with different features.

A server that loaded a bank builds a SteeringBatch on every forward pass, including the ones CompilationManager precompiles and the ones nobody asked to steer. Those carry alpha 0 and a threshold of +inf and fire on no token. That's what holds the executable count at one: forward_batch is a jit argument and ForwardBatch is a registered pytree, so None against a SteeringBatch is a different treedef, and the hook widens the residual stream to float32 in one of the two and not the other. Two treedefs means the precompiled buckets cover the unsteered graph alone and the first steering request pays a full prefill compile. The bank places the all-off arrays once per token count, and a lax.cond on alpha inside the hook skips the two bank gathers and the projection when no token steers, so an all-off pass costs the float32 widening and one reduction over [T].

The bank records the site it was fit at, and load() refuses a bank whose site isn't the --steering-layer the server got. Width can't catch that: every layer of a model is the same width. Capture appends before layer(...) runs and the hook fires after it returns, so capture slot k and --steering-layer k - 1 name the same tensor, and an SAE trained on slot 20 serves at 19. load() also refuses a bank whose thresholds don't match its features, an empty bank, a NaN or -inf threshold and a non-finite vector or probe. Each of those loaded and then stopped the server from the scheduler thread. A +inf threshold marks a dead latent. It loads with a warning that names the feature, because static steering reads no threshold and still adds the vector.

The hook widens the residual stream to float32 and leaves it widened. A bf16 add throws a small steering vector away: at a per-element residual RMS of 300 with 5,376 dim, 99% of the delta comes back bit-identical, and rounding back after the add loses the same 99%. Matmuls downstream stay bf16, because LinearBase asks for preferred_element_type=params_dtype and the MoE dispatch casts its input, so what widens is the residual accumulation alone.

The scheduler writes the per-token configuration on the layout _merge_input_and_positions builds, and resolves nothing: it doesn't hold the bank. ForwardBatch.init_new turns feature ids into bank rows, fills in the fitted threshold where a request asked for it, zeroes a request that named a feature the bank doesn't hold, and places the three per-token arrays with device_array, like every other per-token array it builds. jax.device_put would check each host's copy against every other host first, one allgather per array per forward pass on a multi-host mesh.

Steering changes the KV that every layer above the hook writes, so the tokenizer manager appends the steering to the request's extra_key, the way LoRA appends lora_id. A request reuses a cached prefix only from requests that steer the same way, and a position inside that prefix was steered when the prefix ran. A server with --enable-steering refuses a client extra_key that holds "steering:", the prefix every steered namespace starts with, so no request can name a steered request's namespace and share its KV.

The request validator checks every field, and refuses an alpha or a finite threshold past the float32 range, which the scheduler's float32 arrays would turn into inf. It refuses any key outside feature, alpha, mode, threshold and positions, because the scheduler reads a missing field as its default, and a misspelled positions steered every token. It refuses a threshold beside mode static, which the scheduler served as conditional steering. The tokenizer manager refuses a steering request on a server started without --enable-steering, the way #877 refuses return_hidden_states without its flag. Such a request used to come back unsteered with no error. check_server_args refuses --enable-steering with --speculative-algorithm: the verify pass builds its batch without the per-token steering arrays, so a steered request would steer its prefill and nothing after it. The steering dict is logged at debug level where the tokenizer manager sends it, where the scheduler receives it, where the scheduler lays it out per token, and where ForwardBatch.init_new resolves it against the bank.

Engine.generate and async_generate take steering after return_routed_experts, the last positional parameter, so a call that passes the earlier ones by position binds as it did.

gemma4 first, because its layer carries one residual stream rather than a (hidden, residual) pair, so the hook is one call and nothing to unpack. The same three lines port to any model with an unrolled layer loop.

Independent of #877, and the two apply in either order. Run both to steer and read the per-layer stream in one request.
